// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Build the RootObjectMapper with the desired defaults
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder()
        // 1. Default dynamic date formats – use the standard ES defaults
        .dynamicDateFormats(
                Arrays.asList(
                        "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis",
                        "strict_date_time||epoch_millis",
                        "epoch_millis"
                )
        )
        // 2. Default dynamic templates – empty list means “no custom templates”
        .dynamicTemplates(Collections.emptyList())
        // 3. Enable date detection (true = detect dates automatically)
        .dateDetection(true)
        // 4. Enable numeric detection (true = detect numbers automatically)
        .numericDetection(true)
        // 5. Skip runtime fields – this flag is handled by the serializer, not the mapper
        .build();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Prepare the XContentBuilder instance (string representation)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
String xContentBuilderInstance = "xContentBuilderInstance";   // <-- your actual builder instance

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Prepare the serialization parameters (string representation)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
   The `params` argument is a Java ArrayList that controls how the mapper is
   serialized.  In this case we want:
     • include_defaults  →  include all default values in the output
     • skip_runtime_fields →  omit any runtime fields from the output
*/
String params = "[\"include_defaults\", \"skip_runtime_fields\"]";

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Call the helper function
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
    builder: xContentBuilderInstance,
    params: params
});
```

### What the code does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1️⃣** | Builds a `RootObjectMapper` that already contains the default settings you requested. |
| **2️⃣** | Supplies the `XContentBuilder` instance that will receive the serialized JSON. |
| **3️⃣** | Supplies a string representation of a Java `ArrayList` that tells the serializer to: <br>• Include default values (`include_defaults`) <br>• Skip runtime fields (`skip_runtime_fields`). |
| **4️⃣** | Calls the helper function `RootObjectMapper_doXContent`, which writes the mapper’s configuration into the builder according to the supplied parameters. |

> **Note:**  
> - The `params` string must be a valid Java `ArrayList` representation.  In practice you could also construct the list in Java and pass it directly, but the helper expects a *string* representation.  
> - The `xContentBuilderInstance` string is just a placeholder; replace it with your actual `XContentBuilder` instance when you run the code.  

This snippet will produce an XContent (JSON) representation of the `RootObjectMapper` that includes all default settings for dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, and numeric detection, while omitting any runtime fields.

